This happened in the wild, but faster than 'weeks'. npm's registry record for the unscoped package 'postmark-mcp' shows:
- created 2025-09-15T10:44Z;
- 13 versions, 1.0.0 to 1.0.15, published by 2025-09-16T12:41Z (1.0.4 to 1.0.6 are not on record);
- 1.0.16 published 2025-09-17T08:59Z, then 1.0.17 and 1.0.18 the same morning;
- every version unpublished 2025-09-25T03:31Z.
That is about 46 hours of clean releases, then roughly a week live.

The package was not Postmark's. Postmark's official '@activecampaign/postmark-mcp' first appeared on npm on 2025-11-26.

Not re-verified here (host blocked, search budget exhausted): per Koi Security's 25 Sep 2025 disclosure, 1.0.16 added a single line that BCC'd every outgoing email to an attacker address (phan@giftshop.club), and the package had roughly 1,500 weekly downloads.

Invariant Labs' 2025 tool-shadowing demo produced the same outcome through a poisoned tool description: 'all emails sent by the agent are leaked to the attacker's server'.
